WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.734 --> 00:00:01.600
Hey everybody, hey.

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.840
It’s been five years since Elon Musk&nbsp;
first showed off Tesla’s solar roof tiles.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.120
You’ll want to call your neighbors over&nbsp;
and say, ‘check out this sweet roof.’”

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.000
Yet five years later, they&nbsp;
are far from mainstream.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.040
Unlike Tesla’s other offering&nbsp;
of large solar panels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.240
these tiles are made to look like normal roofing&nbsp;
shingles that generate electricity for your home.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.440
Getting into the business of&nbsp;
making them was controversial.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:30.080
Tesla shareholders sued Musk for buying the&nbsp;
solar panel company founded by his cousins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:34.720
SolarCity, for $2.6 billion alleging&nbsp;
it amounted to a bailout for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.360
family of which. He was also the&nbsp;
largest shareholder of SolarCity.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.600
So Musk bet big on these tiles.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.080
But their deployment has fallen from the&nbsp;
highs of 2017, although it appears to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:51.280
picking up recently. Tesla notes 2021 saw a 68%&nbsp;
increase in megawatts deployed year over year.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:56.000
However, although Tesla claimed it could&nbsp;
manufacture 1,000 solar tile roofs a week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.400
Bloomberg reported that the company could&nbsp;
barely manage 200 weekly installs in 2021.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.000
I’ve reached out to Tesla for the&nbsp;
latest figures but haven't heard back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.920
Turns out, it’s been harder to&nbsp;
install the roofs than Tesla imagined.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:13.040
“…we are choked at the installation point.&nbsp;
We basically made some significant mistakes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:19.360
in assessing the difficulty of certain roofs.&nbsp;
The complexity of roofs varies dramatically. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.000
you just can’t have a one&nbsp;
size fits all situation.”

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.960
Tesla installs some roofs themselves and at&nbsp;
other times, relies on third-party installers.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:30.800
So the big thing with Tesla’s solar&nbsp;
roof is the complexity factor.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.760
CJ McConaghy is the owner of Three Tree Roofing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:36.880
a certified Tesla roof&nbsp;
installer in the Seattle area.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:41.200
The plans that we get, that we design&nbsp;
for the roof, it looks like a lego kit.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.880
Just like a lego set, all the pieces&nbsp;
have to fit together perfectly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.200
The wiring is also very complex.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:52.000
This isn’t something we deal with with any&nbsp;
other kind of roof. Dealing with the tiles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.440
handling them because it’s glass.&nbsp;
They’re also pretty slippery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:00.080
There are just a lot more moving pieces to&nbsp;
this type of roofing than a traditional roof.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.320
So if a traditional roof, the kind&nbsp;
that most houses currently have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:08.160
takes three days to install,&nbsp;
how long does a Tesla roof take?

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:13.200
That’s a great question.&nbsp;
If it’s a moderate to easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.920
roof design the Tesla solar roof is&nbsp;
going to be more like 10 to 12 days.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:23.840
Given the unforeseen complexities, Tesla hiked the&nbsp;
prices of its roofs which really upset customers.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:27.200
And got slapped with a class-action&nbsp;
lawsuit in California last year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:34.400
led by a customer who said his contract got&nbsp;
increased from $71,000 to a whopping $146,000!

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.720
Tesla then told customers’ attorneys it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.000
reverse the hike for those who&nbsp;
had already signed contracts.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.360
So, how much could you expect to pay&nbsp;
for one of these roofs right now?

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:48.000
That depends on many factors: where you&nbsp;
live, the size of your home, its age.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:53.040
As an example, Tesla’s order page shows a&nbsp;
2000-square foot home in Las Vegas with an average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:03:00.640
electricity bill of $135 a month is estimated to&nbsp;
cost $57,300 before the U.S. federal tax credit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:05.600
Tesla notes the price can vary up&nbsp;
to $12,000 depending on complexity.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:08.160
To try to entice more people&nbsp;
to choose their roofs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:13.920
Tesla recently announced a 20-year loan option&nbsp;
so customers can pay for their system over time.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:17.440
A big reason Tesla hasn’t done more&nbsp;
to boost its solar business – is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:21.600
because its primary focus is on&nbsp;
mass-producing electric cars.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:27.360
Tesla went through hell in 2018 trying to churn&nbsp;
out its most affordable car yet, the Model 3.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.720
So it was all hands on deck to crank out as many&nbsp;
cars as possible and solar took a back seat.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:35.040
“We had to basically take the whole&nbsp;
company including a bunch of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.920
that were on solar (and) have them work on cars.”

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.240
Not only did Tesla divert staff&nbsp;
it also fired solar executives.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:47.040
Bloomberg reported that Musk dismissed&nbsp;
both the head of Tesla Energy, RJ Johnson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:52.480
and Ryan Nungesser, the director of operations at&nbsp;
Tesla’s Buffalo factory where the tiles are made.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:58.640
And now, the focus is on Model Y production&nbsp;
at two new gigafactories: in Texas and Berlin.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:03.920
Even with so much on its plate, Tesla&nbsp;
likely won’t give up on its solar ambitions.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:07.120
Because it’s a way to show the world&nbsp;
that it’s more than a car company but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:10.160
one that intends to lead the&nbsp;
transition to clean energy.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:11.680
And I hope you agree that’s the future we want.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:17.040
Solar is an integral part of&nbsp;
its clean energy ecosystem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.320
The energy produced from the roof&nbsp;
is stored in the Tesla powerwall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:23.680
which can power your home during an&nbsp;
outage or charge your electric car.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:26.240
It’s an obvious three-part solution.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:28.880
Except two parts are still missing.

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:30.400
Not only the solar tiles.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.680
But also, powerwall production&nbsp;
has lagged behind which Musk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:36.240
blames on chip shortages due to the pandemic.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:40.320
Tesla acknowledges its solar division has&nbsp;
work to do. It wrote in its fourth-quarter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:43.760
earnings report that it plans on&nbsp;
“...making further cost improvements…”

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:45.840
It appears to be making&nbsp;
progress in that direction.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:50.880
Tesla has launched an updated solar roof&nbsp;
tile with a 23% higher power capacity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.200
without increasing the tile size according&nbsp;
to a spec sheet obtained by Electrek.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.560
So fewer solar tiles would be&nbsp;
needed since each roof uses a mix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:03.280
of tiles - some with and without solar&nbsp;
cells although they all look the same.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:08.000
Fewer solar tiles mean fewer electrical&nbsp;
connections which can bring down the cost.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:13.600
Although Tesla’s solar roofs have yet to take&nbsp;
off, Musk says he’s confident it will happen.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:17.120
I think it will not be long before Tesla&nbsp;
is by far the market leader in solar.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:20.880
